## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow + cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `Thread.Join(timeout)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to loop condition,
                // which will now exit because token is cancelled.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish cleanly
        if (_workerThread is not null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
            {
                // Thread didn't exit in time — log a warning.
                // In .NET 8 there is NO way to forcibly kill it.
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key change: `GetNextItem` must cooperate

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept a `CancellationToken` — otherwise cancellation stalls until it returns on its own:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when token fires
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
```

If using `BlockingCollection<T>`, its `Take(CancellationToken)` overload handles this natively.

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `CancellationToken` is cooperative — if `GetNextItem()` ignores it, `Stop()` hangs until the call returns naturally. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated cancellations. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers/callbacks. Always wrap in `IDisposable` or `using`. |
| **Expecting cancellation to be instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running. |
| **No fallback for uncooperative code** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, there is **no** forced kill in .NET 8. If third-party code blocks without accepting a token, move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill`. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort` mental model** | There's no equivalent because there's no "pending abort" to reset. The loop simply checks `IsCancellationRequested` and decides whether to exit or continue. |

---

### If you want the thread to survive individual item failures

The original `ResetAbort` pattern let the thread continue processing after an abort. The equivalent in cooperative cancellation is: use a **per-item linked token** so you can cancel one item without killing the loop:

```csharp
while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
{
    using var itemCts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(token);
    itemCts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)); // per-item timeout

    try
    {
        var item = GetNextItem(token);
        ProcessItem(item, itemCts.Token);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Per-item timeout fired, but parent token is still alive — rollback and continue
        RollbackCurrentTransaction();
    }
}
```

This gives you the "abort one item, keep processing" semantics that `ResetAbort` provided, without any deprecated APIs.
